▶️ How to Run the Project

The general syntax is:

./UserManager.sh <operation> <arguments>

⚠️ User and group management operations may require sudo or root privileges depending on the implementation.

👥 1. Add a Team

Create a team named amigo:

./UserManager.sh addTeam amigo

Create another team:

./UserManager.sh addTeam unixkings
👤 2. Add a User Under a Team

Add Rakesh to amigo:

./UserManager.sh addUser Rakesh amigo

Add Sandeep to unixkings:

./UserManager.sh addUser Sandeep unixkings
📂 Expected Directory Structure

After creating the users, the structure should be similar to:

/home
│
├── Rakesh
│   ├── team
│   └── ninja
│
└── Sandeep
    ├── team
    └── ninja
🔐 3. Home Directory Permissions

The home directory of every user should follow these access rules:

User Type	Permission
👤 Owner	Read + Write + Execute
👥 Same Team	Read + Execute
🌍 Others	Execute

The required permission model is:

Owner  → rwx
Team   → r-x
Others → --x
🤝 4. Team Shared Directory

The team directory inside each user's home directory is shared with members of the same team.

Example:

/home/Rakesh/team

All members of Rakesh's team should have full access to this directory.

🥷 5. Ninja Shared Directory

The ninja directory is shared among all ninjas.

Example:

/home/Rakesh/ninja

All ninjas should have full access to the directory.

🔑 6. Change User Password

Change Rakesh's password:

./UserManager.sh changePasswd Rakesh

The utility should prompt for the new password.

🐚 7. Change User Shell

Change Rakesh's shell to Bash:

./UserManager.sh changeShell Rakesh /bin/bash
🗑️ 8. Delete a User

Delete Rakesh:

./UserManager.sh delUser Rakesh
👥 9. Delete a Team

Delete the amigo team:

./UserManager.sh delTeam amigo
📋 10. List Users
./UserManager.sh ls User
📋 11. List Teams
./UserManager.sh ls Team
